{{>api_info}}

defmodule {{moduleName}}.Error do
  @moduledoc """
  Branded grouping for every exception this SDK can raise.

  Elixir exceptions are plain `defexception` structs and have no
  inheritance, so there is no literal "root" class the way the
  object-oriented SDKs express it. Instead, membership in the
  "Zitadel SDK error" family is expressed explicitly and
  idiomatically:

    * `exceptions/0` returns the canonical list of every SDK
      exception module. Use it directly in a single `rescue` clause
      to catch *any* SDK-thrown error:

          try do
            {{moduleName}}.SomeApi.some_call(client)
          rescue
            e in {{moduleName}}.Error.exceptions() ->
              # `e` is guaranteed to be a Zitadel SDK error
              handle(e)
          end

    * `zitadel_error?/1` is a membership predicate that returns
      `true` for any SDK exception struct and `false` for anything
      else (including unrelated `RuntimeError`/`ArgumentError`
      raised by the BEAM itself):

          rescue
            e -> if {{moduleName}}.Error.zitadel_error?(e), do: ..., else: reraise(e, __STACKTRACE__)

  The taxonomy mirrors the object-oriented SDKs:

      Zitadel SDK error  (this grouping)
       ├─ {{moduleName}}.ApiError  (message, status_code, response_headers,
       │                            response_body, error_body, cause)
       │   ├─ 4xx: BadRequest(400) Unauthorized(401) Forbidden(403)
       │   │       NotFound(404) Conflict(409) UnprocessableEntity(422)
       │   │       (and the {{moduleName}}.Errors.ClientError grouping)
       │   └─ 5xx: InternalServerError(500)
       │           (and the {{moduleName}}.Errors.ServerError grouping)
       └─ Serialization error ({{moduleName}}.SerializationError and the
                               related {{moduleName}}.SchemaMismatchError /
                               {{moduleName}}.DeserializationError)

  The list is the single source of truth: every SDK exception module
  must appear in `exceptions/0`, and both the predicate and the type
  union below are derived from it.
  """

  @exceptions [
    {{moduleName}}.ApiError,
    {{moduleName}}.Errors.ClientError,
    {{moduleName}}.Errors.ServerError,
    {{moduleName}}.Errors.BadRequestError,
    {{moduleName}}.Errors.UnauthorizedError,
    {{moduleName}}.Errors.ForbiddenError,
    {{moduleName}}.Errors.NotFoundError,
    {{moduleName}}.Errors.ConflictError,
    {{moduleName}}.Errors.UnprocessableEntityError,
    {{moduleName}}.Errors.InternalServerError,
    {{moduleName}}.SerializationError,
    {{moduleName}}.SchemaMismatchError,
    {{moduleName}}.DeserializationError
  ]

  @type t ::
          {{moduleName}}.ApiError.t()
          | {{moduleName}}.Errors.ClientError.t()
          | {{moduleName}}.Errors.ServerError.t()
          | {{moduleName}}.Errors.BadRequestError.t()
          | {{moduleName}}.Errors.UnauthorizedError.t()
          | {{moduleName}}.Errors.ForbiddenError.t()
          | {{moduleName}}.Errors.NotFoundError.t()
          | {{moduleName}}.Errors.ConflictError.t()
          | {{moduleName}}.Errors.UnprocessableEntityError.t()
          | {{moduleName}}.Errors.InternalServerError.t()
          | Exception.t()

  @doc """
  Returns the canonical list of every exception module this SDK can
  raise. Suitable for use directly in a `rescue e in ... ->` clause
  to catch any Zitadel SDK error.
  """
  @spec exceptions() :: [module()]
  def exceptions, do: @exceptions

  @doc """
  Returns `true` if `term` is an instance of any Zitadel SDK
  exception, `false` otherwise.
  """
  @spec zitadel_error?(term()) :: boolean()
  def zitadel_error?(%module{}), do: module in @exceptions
  def zitadel_error?(_term), do: false
end
